BT 1 0 0 1 0 72.5 Tm 11.5 TL /F2 8 Tf .176471 .215686 .282353 rg (1. The 'Angina Paradox' \(`cp`\) & Catheterization Referral Bias:) Tj /F1 8 Tf  T* (In medical textbooks, typical angina \(retrosternal pressure radiating to the left arm\) is considered the hallmark of coronary stenosis. However, our) Tj T* (contingency analysis reveals that ) Tj /F2 8 Tf (Asymptomatic patients \(Category 4\) exhibited the highest disease prevalence \(72.9%\)) Tj /F1 8 Tf (, while Typical) Tj T* (Angina patients had only 30.4% prevalence \($\\chi^2 = 81.82, p < 10^{-17}$\). This counterintuitive finding is a classic example of ) Tj /F2 8 Tf (referral bias in) Tj T* (tertiary catheterization laboratories) Tj /F1 8 Tf (: patients presenting with typical angina were frequently managed medically without invasive catheterization,) Tj T* (whereas asymptomatic individuals were referred for invasive catheterization only when prior non-invasive tests \(such as severe treadmill ST) Tj T* (depression or perfusion defects\) had already flagged them as extraordinarily high-risk.) Tj T* ET
